  "notes": "Early-90s teen-to-adult R&B-pop in the Narada Michael Walden orbit, 1991–1999. The research gives \"54–93; the uptempo is 93 straight, the ballads 54–72 half-time\", and the uptempo is encoded because it is the half the entry describes as a kit — \"a bright NJS-adjacent kit with a swung 16th hat, snare on 2&4, hand-claps\" — while the ballads are stated as \"kick-on-1/snare-on-3 with no hats\", a different backbeat class not folded in here. The stated ballad top of 72 fixes the straight complement at 73–93 exactly, and the mode is the entry's own uptempo reading of 93. The three drum deltas are that swung 16th hat (0.60 swing, continuous, fill density 0.56 with the open hat on both offbeats), a clap stack on the backbeat with ghost activity on `2e` and `4a`, and a tambourine-led perc bed. Harmony is \"jazz-inflected major harmony\", so the families are ii–V–I and I–vi–ii–V rather than modal vamps. The identifying melodic parameter is range: \"wide melodic range with whistle-register ad-libs as the identity, high note-density in the runs\" — the melody spans thirty-two semitones to MIDI 96, the highest ceiling in this section, with the highest leap share and octave-jump probability in this batch and a 4–11 density. ⚠ Confidence is **low**: the research rates the entry medium-low, says only the tempos are sourced, and marks both the key claim and the saxophone hook `[LOW-CONF]` — the sax is carried as a counter at −12 with `answer_lick` leading rather than as a named instrument. ⚠ Every velocity, density and register number is INFERRED. ⚠ `session.keys` is INFERRED: the entry's Keys line gives \"major-mode on the uptempos, minor on the ballads [LOW-CONF]\" and enumerates no tonic, so the values here and their weights are this author's reading of that line.",
